/-!
# The two `Ō`-module structures on `Λ_• = B̲ ⊗^L_𝒪 Ō`, and the base change they do not support

Let `A → B` be a ring map and let `C_{B/A}` be the polynomial site, on the small model
`CommAlgCat.smallBoundedPolynomialSite A B κ`. Write `𝒪` for the structure presheaf,
`Ō = 𝒪 ⊗_A B̲` and `B̲` for the constant presheaf `B`, so that over an object `U = (S, g)` of
the small model the sections are

`𝒪(U) = A[S]`,  `Ō(U) = B[S]`,  `B̲(U) = B`,

the augmentation `Ō(U) → B` is evaluation at `g : S → B`, and `𝒦(U)` is the ideal generated by
the elements `X s - g s` (see `CommAlgCat.smallPolynomialQuotientAlgEquiv`). Nothing in that
setup asks `A → B` to be surjective.

This file settles the status of the *functorial base change for the tower `𝒪 → Ō → B̲`* that the
pro-nilpotence hypothesis `CommAlgCat.SmallPolynomialAugmentationProNilpotent` was to be derived
from, namely the asserted identification

`𝒦ⁿ ⊗^L_𝒪 B̲ ≅ Λ_• ⊗^L_B (𝒦ⁿ ⊗^L_Ō B̲)`,  `Λ_• = B̲ ⊗^L_𝒪 Ō`.    (★)

**(★) is false.** It is false already at a single object of the site, in degree one, for the
simplest possible coefficients. This file produces the counterexample.

## Why it fails, and what the two `Ō`-structures are

Writing `R_• → B̲` for a K-flat resolution over `𝒪` and using that `𝒪` is flat over `A̲`,

`Λ_• = Ō ⊗_𝒪 R_• = (𝒪 ⊗_A B) ⊗_𝒪 R_• = B ⊗_A R_• ≃ B ⊗^L_A B̲`,

so `Λ_•` is a module over `Ō ⊗_𝒪 B̲ = B ⊗_A B` and the two `Ō`-structures on it are the two ring
maps `Ō → B ⊗_A B`:

* the **`Ō`-factor structure**, `b ↦ b ⊗ 1` and `X s ↦ 1 ⊗ g s`, which is the one `Λ_•` carries
  as `Ō ⊗^L_𝒪 B̲`, and the one for which the change-of-rings isomorphism
  `𝒦ⁿ ⊗^L_𝒪 B̲ ≅ Λ_• ⊗^L_Ō 𝒦ⁿ` holds; and
* the **`B̲`-factor structure**, `b ↦ 1 ⊗ b` and `X s ↦ 1 ⊗ g s`, which is the restriction along
  `Ō → B̲` of a `B̲`-structure, and the one the right-hand side of (★) presupposes, since
  `Λ_• ⊗^L_B W ≅ Λ_• ⊗^L_Ō W` for a `B̲`-complex `W`.

The two agree on the variables and differ on `B`, by the flip of `B ⊗_A B`. So they agree as soon
as `B ⊗_A B = B`, that is as soon as `A → B` is **surjective** — which is exactly the hypothesis
of [Stacks, Tag 08RF], and exactly what lets the source choose every object `U = (P → B)` in the
form `P = A[E]` with every variable mapping to `0`. It is also the same obstruction as the one
recorded for the change-of-rings spectral sequence of [Stacks, Tag 068F], where `X e - g e` acts
on `Tor_0^𝒪(B, Ō) = B ⊗_A B` as `1 ⊗ g e - g e ⊗ 1`. That the two derived structures are
genuinely different is [Stacks, Tag 08YT]
(`CochainComplex.DerivedTensor.derivedTensorModuleStructuresDiffer`).

## The counterexample

Take `A = k` for any nontrivial commutative ring `k`, take `B = k[S]` the polynomial ring on a set
`S` with at least two elements, and take for `U` the object `(S, g)` with `g s = X s`, that is the
object whose augmentation `𝒪(U) → B̲(U)` is the identity. Then, at that object:

* `𝒪(U) → B̲(U)` is an isomorphism, so `B̲(U)` is free of rank one over `𝒪(U)` and
  `Tor_i^{𝒪(U)}(B̲, 𝒦) = 0` for every `i ≥ 1`: the left-hand side of (★) is concentrated in
  degree `0`. This is `MvPolynomial.isZero_tor_succ_of_bijective_augmentation`.
* `Ō(U) = B ⊗_k B` and `𝒦(U)` is its diagonal ideal, and
  `Tor_1^{Ō(U)}(B̲, 𝒦) ≠ 0`. This is `MvPolynomial.not_isZero_tor_one_augmentationQuotient`:
  the dimension shift `Stacks.Tor.DimensionShift.torOneIdealIsoKerMulMap` identifies that `Tor`
  with the kernel of multiplication `𝒦 ⊗_Ō 𝒦 → Ō`, and the Koszul class
  `(X i - g i) ⊗ (X j - g j) - (X j - g j) ⊗ (X i - g i)` is a nonzero element of that kernel,
  being detected by the pairing `a ⊗ b ↦ ∂ᵢa(g) · ∂ⱼb(g)`.
* `Ō(U)` is free over `𝒪(U)` and `Λ_•(U) = B̲ ⊗_{𝒪(U)} Ō(U)` is free and nonzero over `B`, so
  `Λ_•(U)` is concentrated in degree `0` and `- ⊗_B Λ_•(U)` is faithful.

Hence the right-hand side of (★) has `H_{-1} = Λ_•(U) ⊗_B Tor_1^{Ō(U)}(𝒦, B̲) ≠ 0` while the
left-hand side has `H_{-1} = Tor_1^{𝒪(U)}(𝒦, B̲) = 0`. The two are not isomorphic, in any
category, and no choice of comparison map can help.

The failure is not an artefact of `n = 1`: it is the failure of the second `Ō`-structure to exist,
which is independent of `n`.

## Main results

* `MvPolynomial.not_isZero_tor_one_augmentationQuotient`: `Tor_1^{B[S]}(B, 𝒦) ≠ 0` for the
  augmentation ideal `𝒦` at any point `g`, as soon as `S` has two distinct elements and `B` is
  nontrivial. This is the nonvanishing that drives the counterexample, and it is also the reason
  the source's dévissage needs the graded pieces: `𝒦` is nowhere near flat over `Ō`.
* `MvPolynomial.isZero_tor_succ_of_bijective_augmentation`: at an object whose augmentation
  `𝒪(U) → B̲(U)` is bijective, such as the diagonal point `g = X` of `B = k[S]`, all the higher
  `Tor` over `𝒪(U)` against `B̲(U)` vanish.
* `MvPolynomial.diagonalAug_comp_diagonalCoeffMap` and
  `MvPolynomial.bijective_diagonalAugmentation`: at the diagonal object the augmentation
  `𝒪(U) ⟶ B̲(U)` is the identity of `k[S]`. It is a retraction of the coefficient map
  `𝒪(U) ⟶ Ō(U)`, which is what makes `- ⊗_B Λ_•(U)` faithful.
* `MvPolynomial.augmentationIdeal_ne_bot`: `𝒦(U) ≠ 0`, which is the obstruction in its sharpest
  form — at the diagonal object `Λ_•(U) ≅ Ō(U)` is free of rank one over `Ō(U)`, so `𝒦` does not
  annihilate it, while `𝒦` annihilates every presheaf of `B̲`-modules.
* `MvPolynomial.baseChange_diagonal_counterexample`: the three facts bundled.

The base change that *does* hold for the tower, and is functorial, is the transitivity
`K ⊗^L_𝒪 B̲ ≅ (K ⊗^L_𝒪 Ō) ⊗^L_Ō B̲` of
`Stacks.ChaoticSite.derivedExtendScalarsComp`. The `(★)`-shaped statement with `Λ_•` carrying its
correct, `Ō`-factor, structure — `𝒦ⁿ ⊗^L_𝒪 B̲ ≅ Λ_• ⊗^L_Ō 𝒦ⁿ` — is true but cannot presently be
written down in this library: it needs a derived tensor product of two variables on
`D(Mod(Ō))`, which the module docstring of
`StacksFrontier/CategoryTheory/Sites/Cohomology/CategoryHomology/ExternalTensorProduct.lean`
records as not existing.

## What this does and does not say about `hpro`

`CommAlgCat.SmallPolynomialAugmentationProNilpotent` is a statement about the derived colimit over
the whole site, not about one object, so nothing here refutes it. What is refuted is the route to
it through (★). The route survives verbatim once `A → B` is assumed surjective, which is the
setting of [Stacks, Tag 08RF]; the repository's `hpro` is stated without that hypothesis, and for
a non-surjective `A → B` the identification (★) that the route needs is unavailable. `hpro`
remains unproved.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 08RF](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08RF)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 08RE](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08RE)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 08YT](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08YT)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits
open scoped TensorProduct

universe u

namespace MvPolynomial

/-! ### The augmentation ideal and its Koszul class -/

section Augmentation

variable {B : Type u} [CommRing B] {σ : Type u} (g : σ → B)

/-! The augmentation ideal `𝒦(U) = ker(Ō(U) → B̲(U))` at the point `g` is
`MvPolynomial.augmentationIdeal` of `StacksFrontier/RingTheory/AugmentationIdealGraded.lean`, the
kernel of `aeval g`; this file imports it rather than restating it. -/

/-- The generators `X s - g s` of the augmentation ideal lie in it. -/
lemma X_sub_C_mem_augmentationIdeal (s : σ) :
    X s - C (g s) ∈ augmentationIdeal g := by
  simp [mem_augmentationIdeal]

/-- The generator `X i - g i` of the augmentation ideal is nonzero: its coefficient at the
monomial `X i` is `1`. -/
lemma X_sub_C_ne_zero [Nontrivial B] (i : σ) :
    (X i - C (g i) : MvPolynomial σ B) ≠ 0 := by
  classical
  intro h
  have h3 : (Finsupp.single i 1 : σ →₀ ℕ) ≠ 0 := by simp [Finsupp.single_eq_zero]
  have h1 : coeff (Finsupp.single i 1) (X i - C (g i) : MvPolynomial σ B) = 1 := by
    rw [coeff_sub, coeff_X_same, coeff_C, if_neg (fun hh => h3 hh.symm), sub_zero]
  rw [h, coeff_zero] at h1
  exact zero_ne_one h1

/-- **The augmentation ideal is nonzero.** This is the obstruction in its sharpest form: at the
diagonal object `Λ_•` is free of rank one over `Ō(U)`, so `𝒦(U)` does not annihilate it, whereas
`𝒦` annihilates every presheaf of `B̲`-modules. So `Λ_•` does not carry the `B̲`-module structure
that the right-hand side of the asserted identification presupposes. -/
theorem augmentationIdeal_ne_bot [Nontrivial B] (i : σ) :
    augmentationIdeal g ≠ ⊥ := by
  intro h
  have hmem := X_sub_C_mem_augmentationIdeal g i
  rw [h, Ideal.mem_bot] at hmem
  exact X_sub_C_ne_zero g i hmem

/-- `1` is not in the augmentation ideal, so the residue ring `Ō(U)/𝒦(U)` is nontrivial. -/
lemma one_notMem_augmentationIdeal [Nontrivial B] :
    (1 : MvPolynomial σ B) ∉ augmentationIdeal g := by
  simp [mem_augmentationIdeal]

/-- The residue ring `Ō(U)/𝒦(U)` of the augmentation ideal is nontrivial. -/
instance nontrivial_augmentationQuotient [Nontrivial B] :
    Nontrivial (MvPolynomial σ B ⧸ augmentationIdeal g) :=
  ⟨⟨1, 0, fun h => one_notMem_augmentationIdeal g
    ((Ideal.Quotient.eq_zero_iff_mem).1 h)⟩⟩

/-- **The partial derivative at the augmentation point.** For `a` in the augmentation ideal the
value `(∂a/∂X i)(g)` is well defined, and `a ↦ (∂a/∂X i)(g)` is `Ō(U)`-linear from `𝒦(U)` to the
residue ring: the Leibniz rule produces the extra term `a · ∂t/∂X i`, which lies in `𝒦(U)`
because `a` does. -/
def augmentationPderiv (i : σ) :
    augmentationIdeal g →ₗ[MvPolynomial σ B] MvPolynomial σ B ⧸ augmentationIdeal g where
  toFun a := Ideal.Quotient.mk _ (pderiv i (a : MvPolynomial σ B))
  map_add' a b := by
    change Ideal.Quotient.mk _ (pderiv i ((a : MvPolynomial σ B) + (b : MvPolynomial σ B))) = _
    rw [map_add, map_add]
  map_smul' t a := by
    change Ideal.Quotient.mk _ (pderiv i (t * (a : MvPolynomial σ B))) = _
    rw [pderiv_mul, map_add,
      show (Ideal.Quotient.mk (augmentationIdeal g)) (pderiv i t * (a : MvPolynomial σ B)) = 0 from
        (Ideal.Quotient.eq_zero_iff_mem).2 (Ideal.mul_mem_left _ _ a.2),
      zero_add]
    rfl

/-- **The pairing detecting the Koszul class.** It sends `a ⊗ b` to `(∂a/∂X i)(g)·(∂b/∂X j)(g)`
in the residue ring, and is `Ō(U)`-linear because each factor is. -/
def augmentationPairing (i j : σ) :
    (augmentationIdeal g ⊗[MvPolynomial σ B] augmentationIdeal g) →ₗ[MvPolynomial σ B]
      MvPolynomial σ B ⧸ augmentationIdeal g :=
  TensorProduct.lift
      (LinearMap.mul (MvPolynomial σ B) (MvPolynomial σ B ⧸ augmentationIdeal g)) ∘ₗ
    TensorProduct.map (augmentationPderiv g i) (augmentationPderiv g j)

/-- The pairing sends `a ⊗ b` to the product of the residues of `∂a/∂X i` and `∂b/∂X j`. -/
lemma augmentationPairing_tmul (i j : σ) (a b : augmentationIdeal g) :
    augmentationPairing g i j (a ⊗ₜ b) =
      Ideal.Quotient.mk _ (pderiv i (a : MvPolynomial σ B)) *
        Ideal.Quotient.mk _ (pderiv j (b : MvPolynomial σ B)) := by
  simp [augmentationPairing, augmentationPderiv]

/-- **The Koszul class** `(X i - g i) ⊗ (X j - g j) - (X j - g j) ⊗ (X i - g i)` of the
augmentation ideal. -/
def augmentationKoszulClass (i j : σ) :
    augmentationIdeal g ⊗[MvPolynomial σ B] augmentationIdeal g :=
  ⟨X i - C (g i), X_sub_C_mem_augmentationIdeal g i⟩ ⊗ₜ
      ⟨X j - C (g j), X_sub_C_mem_augmentationIdeal g j⟩ -
    ⟨X j - C (g j), X_sub_C_mem_augmentationIdeal g j⟩ ⊗ₜ
      ⟨X i - C (g i), X_sub_C_mem_augmentationIdeal g i⟩

/-- The Koszul class is killed by multiplication `𝒦 ⊗_Ō 𝒦 → Ō`, the ring being commutative. -/
lemma mulMap_augmentationKoszulClass (i j : σ) :
    Submodule.mulMap (augmentationIdeal g) (augmentationIdeal g)
      (augmentationKoszulClass g i j) = 0 := by
  simp [augmentationKoszulClass, Submodule.mulMap_tmul, mul_comm]

/-- The pairing takes the value `1` on the Koszul class: the first summand contributes
`1 · 1` and the second `0 · 0`. -/
lemma augmentationPairing_augmentationKoszulClass {i j : σ} (hij : i ≠ j) :
    augmentationPairing g i j (augmentationKoszulClass g i j) = 1 := by
  rw [augmentationKoszulClass, map_sub, augmentationPairing_tmul, augmentationPairing_tmul]
  simp [pderiv_X_of_ne hij, pderiv_X_of_ne hij.symm]

/-- The Koszul class is nonzero, being detected by the pairing. -/
lemma augmentationKoszulClass_ne_zero [Nontrivial B] {i j : σ} (hij : i ≠ j) :
    augmentationKoszulClass g i j ≠ 0 := by
  intro h
  have := augmentationPairing_augmentationKoszulClass g hij
  rw [h, map_zero] at this
  exact zero_ne_one this

/-- **The kernel of multiplication `𝒦 ⊗_Ō 𝒦 → Ō` is nonzero**, as soon as the site object has two
distinct variables: it contains the Koszul class. -/
theorem nontrivial_ker_mulMap_augmentationIdeal [Nontrivial B] {i j : σ} (hij : i ≠ j) :
    Nontrivial
      (LinearMap.ker (Submodule.mulMap (augmentationIdeal g) (augmentationIdeal g))) :=
  ⟨⟨⟨augmentationKoszulClass g i j, mulMap_augmentationKoszulClass g i j⟩, 0,
    fun h => augmentationKoszulClass_ne_zero g hij (congrArg Subtype.val h)⟩⟩

/-- **The nonvanishing that refutes the base change.** For the augmentation ideal `𝒦(U)` of
`Ō(U) = B[S]` at any point `g`, with `S` carrying two distinct elements and `B` nontrivial,

`Tor_1^{Ō(U)}(B̲, 𝒦(U)) ≠ 0`.

By the dimension shift `Stacks.Tor.DimensionShift.torOneIdealIsoKerMulMap` this `Tor` is the
kernel of multiplication `𝒦 ⊗_Ō 𝒦 → Ō`, which contains the Koszul class of two distinct
variables. -/
theorem not_isZero_tor_one_augmentationQuotient [Nontrivial B] {i j : σ} (hij : i ≠ j) :
    ¬ IsZero (((Tor' (ModuleCat.{u} (MvPolynomial σ B)) 1).obj
        (ModuleCat.of (MvPolynomial σ B)
          (MvPolynomial σ B ⧸ augmentationIdeal g))).obj
      (ModuleCat.of (MvPolynomial σ B) (augmentationIdeal g))) := by
  intro h
  have h' := h.of_iso
    (Stacks.Tor.DimensionShift.torOneIdealIsoKerMulMap (augmentationIdeal g)).symm
  rw [ModuleCat.isZero_iff_subsingleton] at h'
  exact (not_subsingleton_iff_nontrivial.2
    (nontrivial_ker_mulMap_augmentationIdeal g hij)) h'

end Augmentation

/-! ### The left-hand side at an object whose augmentation is invertible -/

section Invertible

variable {A : Type u} [CommRing A] {B : Type u} [CommRing B] {σ : Type u}

/-- If the augmentation `𝒪(U) = A[S] → B̲(U) = B` of a site object is bijective, then `B̲(U)` is
free of rank one as an `𝒪(U)`-module. -/
lemma projective_restrictScalars_of_bijective (ψ : MvPolynomial σ A →+* B)
    (hψ : Function.Bijective ψ) :
    Projective ((ModuleCat.restrictScalars ψ).obj (ModuleCat.of B B)) := by
  let ε : MvPolynomial σ A ≃+* B := RingEquiv.ofBijective ψ hψ
  let l : ((ModuleCat.restrictScalars ψ).obj (ModuleCat.of B B)) ≃ₗ[MvPolynomial σ A]
      MvPolynomial σ A :=
    { toFun := fun b => ε.symm (show B from b)
      map_add' := fun x y => map_add _ (show B from x) (show B from y)
      map_smul' := fun p b => by
        change ε.symm (ψ p * (show B from b)) = p * ε.symm (show B from b)
        rw [map_mul]
        congr 1
        exact ε.symm_apply_apply p
      invFun := fun p => show (ModuleCat.restrictScalars ψ).obj (ModuleCat.of B B) from ε p
      left_inv := fun b => ε.apply_symm_apply (show B from b)
      right_inv := fun p => ε.symm_apply_apply p }
  have : Module.Projective (MvPolynomial σ A)
      ((ModuleCat.restrictScalars ψ).obj (ModuleCat.of B B)) :=
    Module.Projective.of_equiv' (M := MvPolynomial σ A) l.symm
  infer_instance

/-- **The left-hand side of (★) is concentrated in degree zero at an object whose augmentation
`𝒪(U) → B̲` is bijective.** There `B̲` is free of rank one over `𝒪(U) = A[S]`, so every higher
`Tor` over `𝒪(U)` against it vanishes — for every coefficient module whatsoever, in particular
for every `𝒦ⁿ`, uniformly in `n`. -/
theorem isZero_tor_succ_of_bijective_augmentation (ψ : MvPolynomial σ A →+* B)
    (hψ : Function.Bijective ψ) (M : ModuleCat.{u} (MvPolynomial σ A)) (n : ℕ) :
    IsZero (((Tor (ModuleCat.{u} (MvPolynomial σ A)) (n + 1)).obj M).obj
      ((ModuleCat.restrictScalars ψ).obj (ModuleCat.of B B))) :=
  have := projective_restrictScalars_of_bijective ψ hψ
  isZero_Tor_succ_of_projective _ M _ n

end Invertible

/-! ### The counterexample: the diagonal point of `B = k[S]` over `A = k` -/

section Diagonal

variable (k : Type u) [CommRing k] (σ : Type u)

/-- The coefficient map `𝒪(U) = A[S] ⟶ Ō(U) = B[S]` for `A = k` and `B = k[S]`: it sends the
variable `X s` of `𝒪(U)` to the variable `X s` of `Ō(U)`. -/
abbrev diagonalCoeffMap :
    MvPolynomial σ k →+* MvPolynomial σ (MvPolynomial σ k) :=
  MvPolynomial.map (C : k →+* MvPolynomial σ k)

/-- The augmentation `Ō(U) = B[S] ⟶ B̲(U) = B` of the **diagonal object** `U = (S, g)` of the
site, the one with `g s = X s`: evaluation of a polynomial over `B = k[S]` at the variables
of `B`. -/
abbrev diagonalAug :
    MvPolynomial σ (MvPolynomial σ k) →+* MvPolynomial σ k :=
  eval₂Hom (RingHom.id (MvPolynomial σ k)) (X : σ → MvPolynomial σ k)

/-- **At the diagonal object the augmentation `𝒪(U) ⟶ B̲(U)` is the identity of `k[S]`.** Two
consequences, both used: `B̲(U)` is free of rank one over `𝒪(U)`, and the coefficient map
`𝒪(U) ⟶ Ō(U)` is a split injection of `𝒪(U)`-modules, so that `Λ_•(U) = B̲ ⊗_{𝒪(U)} Ō(U)`
contains `B̲` as a `B̲`-module direct summand and `- ⊗_{B̲} Λ_•(U)` is faithful. -/
theorem diagonalAug_comp_diagonalCoeffMap :
    (diagonalAug k σ).comp (diagonalCoeffMap k σ) = RingHom.id (MvPolynomial σ k) :=
  MvPolynomial.ringHom_ext (fun c => by simp) (fun s => by simp)

/-- The augmentation `𝒪(U) ⟶ B̲(U)` of the diagonal object is bijective. -/
theorem bijective_diagonalAugmentation :
    Function.Bijective ((diagonalAug k σ).comp (diagonalCoeffMap k σ)) := by
  rw [diagonalAug_comp_diagonalCoeffMap]
  exact Function.bijective_id

/-- **The functorial base change `𝒦ⁿ ⊗^L_𝒪 B̲ ≅ Λ_• ⊗^L_B (𝒦ⁿ ⊗^L_Ō B̲)` is false.**

Take `A = k` nontrivial, `B = k[S]` with `S` carrying two distinct elements, and let `U` be the
diagonal object `(S, g)` of `C_{B/A}`, the one with `g s = X s`. The three conclusions are, in
order:

1. the augmentation `𝒪(U) ⟶ B̲(U)` is the identity of `k[S]`;
2. consequently `Tor_i^{𝒪(U)}(M, B̲) = 0` for every `i ≥ 1` and every `𝒪(U)`-module `M` — in
   particular for `M = 𝒦ⁿ`, uniformly in `n`, so the left-hand side is concentrated in degree
   zero, as is `Λ_•(U) = B̲ ⊗^L_{𝒪(U)} Ō(U) ≅ Ō(U)`;
3. but `Tor_1^{Ō(U)}(B̲, 𝒦) ≠ 0`.

By (1) the coefficient map `𝒪(U) ⟶ Ō(U)` is split as a map of `𝒪(U) ≅ B̲`-modules, so `Λ_•(U)`
has `B̲` as a `B̲`-module direct summand and therefore
`H_{-1}(Λ_•(U) ⊗^L_B (𝒦 ⊗^L_{Ō(U)} B̲)) = Λ_•(U) ⊗_B Tor_1^{Ō(U)}(𝒦, B̲) ≠ 0` by (3), while
`H_{-1}(𝒦 ⊗^L_{𝒪(U)} B̲) = 0` by (2). The two sides of the asserted identification are therefore
not isomorphic.

What fails is the existence of the second `Ō`-module structure on `Λ_•`, which is independent of
`n`; see the module docstring. The identification is restored by assuming `A ⟶ B` surjective,
which is the hypothesis of [Stacks, Tag 08RF] and is absent from
`CommAlgCat.SmallPolynomialAugmentationProNilpotent`. -/
theorem baseChange_diagonal_counterexample [Nontrivial k] {i j : σ} (hij : i ≠ j) :
    (diagonalAug k σ).comp (diagonalCoeffMap k σ) = RingHom.id (MvPolynomial σ k) ∧
    (∀ (M : ModuleCat.{u} (MvPolynomial σ k)) (n : ℕ),
      IsZero (((Tor (ModuleCat.{u} (MvPolynomial σ k)) (n + 1)).obj M).obj
        ((ModuleCat.restrictScalars ((diagonalAug k σ).comp (diagonalCoeffMap k σ))).obj
          (ModuleCat.of (MvPolynomial σ k) (MvPolynomial σ k))))) ∧
    ¬ IsZero (((Tor' (ModuleCat.{u} (MvPolynomial σ (MvPolynomial σ k))) 1).obj
        (ModuleCat.of (MvPolynomial σ (MvPolynomial σ k))
          (MvPolynomial σ (MvPolynomial σ k) ⧸
            augmentationIdeal (X : σ → MvPolynomial σ k)))).obj
      (ModuleCat.of (MvPolynomial σ (MvPolynomial σ k))
        (augmentationIdeal (X : σ → MvPolynomial σ k)))) :=
  ⟨diagonalAug_comp_diagonalCoeffMap k σ,
    fun M n => isZero_tor_succ_of_bijective_augmentation _
      (bijective_diagonalAugmentation k σ) M n,
    not_isZero_tor_one_augmentationQuotient (X : σ → MvPolynomial σ k) hij⟩

end Diagonal

